<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Empires of the Steppe</h1><p class="path-tagline">How mounted nomads built some of the largest states in history, from the Xiongnu to Timur.</p><p class="path-intro">The grasslands stretching from Mongolia to the Black Sea produced a distinctive kind of empire: mobile, built on horses and herds, held together by charismatic leaders and tribute rather than bureaucracy. Steppe powers repeatedly humbled settled neighbours, extracted silk and grain from them and controlled the trade passing between them. This path traces that tradition from the Xiongnu confederation that confronted Han China, through the Turkic khaganates, to Genghis Khan's world empire and its heirs.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/xiongnu-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luoyang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 318 BCE – 36 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Xiongnu Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The first great steppe empire of eastern Eurasia, united by Modu Chanyu around 209 BCE, forced Han China into decades of appeasement.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/heqin"><span class="path-step-ph">2</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Heqin</span><span class="path-step-why">The 'peace through kinship' arrangement of annual gifts and Han brides that bought peace from the Xiongnu after the Battle of Baideng.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/gokturk-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-wei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 546 CE – 744 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Gokturk Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by Bumin in 552, the first state to bear the name Türk managed Silk Road commerce and left the Orkhon Inscriptions.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/khazar-khaganate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 650 CE – 969 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Khazar Khaganate</span><span class="path-step-why">A western steppe power poised between Byzantium and the caliphate, notable for its religious pluralism and its rulers' conversion to Judaism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · born 1162</span><span class="path-step-name">Genghis Khan</span><span class="path-step-why">Temüjin unified the rival Mongol and Turkic tribes and was proclaimed Genghis Khan at a kurultai in 1206.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mongol-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1206 CE – 1368 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mongol Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Decimal units, promotion by merit and the yam relay system carried Mongol armies from China to Legnica and Mohi by 1241.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/yasa"><span class="path-step-ph">7</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Yasa</span><span class="path-step-why">The body of Mongol customary law that Genghis Khan's heirs, Chagatai above all, enforced across the conquered lands.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/chagatai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaidu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1225 CE – 1500 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Chagatai Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The Central Asian appanage of Genghis Khan's second son shows the lasting tension between nomadic tradition and the sedentary Silk Road cities.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/timur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Timur</span><span class="path-step-why">Rising within the Chagatai Khanate, Timur used the Mongol framework of legitimacy to build an empire from Anatolia to the borders of China.</span></span></a></li></ol>
